{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's entire evidentiary criterion rests on embedding a 'plausible but wrong' decoy item *in the checklist itself*—i.e., the instrument contains a deliberately false step. But the problem asks for evidence that distinguishes reliability gain from compliance 'within a single evaluation' of the *actual task*. A decoy-laced checklist is no longer the same instrument: by construction, panels who trust the checklist's authority may dutifully comply with the false step while still being perfectly reliable on the genuine task, so 'flagging the decoy' tracks skepticism toward the instrument, not reliability on the task. Conversely, a panel that complies with the decoy has only failed an artificially inserted trap, not the real judgment. The criterion thus conflates meta-suspicion of the tool with domain reliability, so it cannot isolate the reliability gain the problem asks about.","counterexample":null,"target_alias":"SRC_001"}]}